// A spread that could replace a field written before it.
//
// Shape members apply left to right, so `...r` wins over the `.tag` above it.
// `r`'s fields are not known where `defaulting` is defined, so nothing here can
// say whether it carries one — width subtyping means a value typed
// `{ .x = Int; }` may carry a `.tag` too. Contributing no names for the spread
// and keeping `.tag = 1` would type this shape `{ .tag = 1; }` for a call that
// hands it `{ .tag = "hi"; }`, which is not a type too wide but a type the run
// disagrees with.
//
// So the shape is refused where it is written. The default-then-override idiom
// this reads as needs a type that says "`.tag` is `r`'s if `r` has one and `1`
// otherwise", and that is presence polymorphism rather than the width
// subtyping this lattice has. Writing the spread first — `{ ...r; .tag = 1; }`
// — is decided, and so is naming the fields wanted from `r`.

open import "blot:prelude"

let defaulting = fn r => { .tag = 1; ...r; }

let tagged = defaulting { .tag = "hi"; }

return tagged.tag
